				<article id="post-1384" class="entry content-bg single-entry post-1384 post type-post status-publish format-standard has-post-thumbnail hentry category-laundry-room">
	<div class="entry-content-wrap">
		
<div class="entry-content single-content">
	
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Laundry closet ideas are for homes where the washer and dryer did not get a room of their own. They live behind a pair of doors in the hallway, in a corner of the bathroom or at the end of the kitchen, and every inch around them has to work. The good news is that a closet is often enough for a perfectly pleasant laundry setup, as long as the machines fit, the air can move and the storage goes up instead of out.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">This guide starts with the measurements that decide what fits, then walks through 18 ideas for doors, hallway closets, tiny closets, closets in other rooms and smart storage. If you have a full room to work with, our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/small-laundry-room-ideas/">small laundry room ideas</a> guide goes deeper on layouts.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/laundry-closet-01-hallway-louvered-doors.jpg" alt="Laundry closet ideas: a hallway laundry closet with cream louvered double doors open, a front-loading washer and dryer under a white oak counter and oak shelves with baskets on a sage green wall" class="wp-image-1375" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/laundry-closet-01-hallway-louvered-doors.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/laundry-closet-01-hallway-louvered-doors-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/laundry-closet-01-hallway-louvered-doors-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/laundry-closet-01-hallway-louvered-doors-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<div class="wp-block-group dsh-summary has-background" style="border-radius:12px;border-left-color:#8FA689;border-left-width:5px;background-color:#EEF3EC;padding-top:20px;padding-right:24px;padding-bottom:20px;padding-left:24px"><div class="wp-block-group__inner-container is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-0d2d72cd wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Key Takeaways</strong></p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Quick answer:</strong> The best laundry closet ideas start with the right fit (about 57 inches wide for side-by-side front-loaders, 29 inches for a stacked full-size pair or 26 inches for a stacked compact pair), then add vented doors, a counter or shelf above the machines and slim storage on the side walls.</li>



<li>Leave about 1 inch at each side and 6 inches behind the machines for hoses and the dryer vent, and make sure the closet doors can open fully.</li>



<li>Use the height: stacking, shelves above the machines and a hanging rod do more in a closet than any bin on the floor.</li>
</ul>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Time:</strong> a weekend (longer for new hookups or a pocket door) · <strong>Cost:</strong> $50–1,500, more if you add hookups · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Easy to moderate</p>
</div></div>


